<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You'll get instant feedback and can retake it as many times as you like.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz">
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> <div><p>Sartre's lecture "Existentialism Is a Humanism" opens with the claim that "existence precedes essence." What does he mean by this?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">Humans are defined by their biological nature before they are born.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">There is no pre-given human nature — we first exist, then define ourselves through our choices.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">The physical world existed before human consciousness came into being.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">Our existence in the world is more certain than our understanding of who we are.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> <div><p>Sartre uses the example of a paper-knife (a letter opener) to illustrate "essence precedes existence." What point is he making?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">Manufactured objects are more reliable than human beings because their purpose is fixed.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">For artifacts, the maker's conception (essence) precedes and determines the thing's existence — but humans have no maker who designed them with a purpose.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">Human beings, like paper-knives, are defined by the uses society assigns to them.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">God created humans the way an artisan creates tools — with a definite purpose in mind.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> <div><p>When Sartre says humans are "condemned to be free," what does he mean?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">Freedom is a punishment — humans would prefer to have their choices made for them.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">We cannot choose not to choose — even refusing to decide is itself a decision — so freedom is inescapable and with it comes total responsibility.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">Political freedom is the highest human value, and we are obligated to fight for it.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">God condemned humans to freedom as a consequence of the Fall.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> <div><p>Sartre describes a waiter who plays at being a waiter — mimicking the role with exaggerated precision. What does this example illustrate?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">Some people are naturally suited to service roles and should embrace them.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">Professional identity is a form of self-knowledge that Sartre admires.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">Bad faith — the self-deception of pretending that one's role or situation determines one's identity, denying one's radical freedom.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">The alienation of labor under capitalism, as Marx described it.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> <div><p>According to Sartre, what is "bad faith" (<em>mauvaise foi</em>)?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">Dishonesty toward others — lying to avoid consequences.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">A religious concept referring to insincere belief or hypocrisy.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">The self-deception by which a person denies their own freedom, treating themselves as a fixed thing rather than a free being.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">Acting against one's own moral principles under social pressure.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> <div><p>Sartre responds to critics who call existentialism "quietist" — meaning it leads to passive acceptance. What is his response?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">He accepts the charge but argues that individual authenticity is more important than political action.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">He denies it: since humans are what they do, existentialism demands action — there is no human nature apart from what we make of ourselves through engagement.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">He agrees that existentialism is primarily a private philosophy and not a guide to political life.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">He argues that political quietism is itself an authentic choice and therefore acceptable.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> <div><p>Sartre argues that in choosing for ourselves, we also choose for all humanity. What does he mean by this?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">Our individual choices automatically change social norms and institutions for everyone.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">When we choose, we implicitly endorse an image of what a human being should be — and this image applies universally, making every choice a statement about what all people ought to do.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">Sartre believes in a collective human will that overrides individual decision-making.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">We are morally required to consult others before making major decisions.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> <div><p>What does Sartre mean when he says that "anguish" is the characteristic feeling of authentic existence?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">Authentic people suffer more than those in bad faith because they see reality clearly.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">Anguish is the recognition that our choices are total and that there is no external authority — God, nature, or reason — that relieves us of responsibility.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">Existentialism produces psychological distress, which Sartre sees as unavoidable but regrettable.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">The anguish of freedom can be overcome through political commitment and solidarity.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> <div><p>Sartre uses the example of a student torn between staying to care for his mother and leaving to join the French Resistance. What does this case demonstrate about his ethics?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">Family obligations always outweigh political ones in Sartre's view.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">Ethical theories like Kantianism can resolve such dilemmas if applied correctly.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">No moral system can make the choice for us — the student must choose, and in choosing, creates the values by which the choice is made; only after acting does its meaning become clear.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">Resistance to fascism is always the right choice because it serves humanity.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> <div><p>Sartre says that existentialism is a form of humanism. How does he define the humanism he has in mind?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">Humanism holds that human beings are the measure of all things and that reason alone guides ethics.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">Humanism celebrates human achievement and the dignity of human culture across history.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">The humanism Sartre endorses is the view that humans are self-creating — there is no fixed human essence, only what we make of ourselves — and that this places unlimited responsibility on each person.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">Humanism is the belief that humans can perfect themselves through education and rational self-improvement.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q11a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">11</span> <div><p>One major objection to Sartre's existentialism is that radical freedom ignores the constraints placed on individuals by their social circumstances — race, class, poverty. What is the force of this objection?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11a" id="q11a">
        <label for="q11a">Sartre's view implies that everyone has equal freedom, which ignores how structural inequalities limit real choices.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11b" id="q11b">
        <label for="q11b">Sartre denies that social circumstances exist, which is empirically false.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11c" id="q11c">
        <label for="q11c">The objection only applies to Sartre's early work; his later writings incorporate social constraints.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11d" id="q11d">
        <label for="q11d">The objection is based on a misreading — Sartre explicitly acknowledges economic limits on freedom.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q12c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">12</span> <div><p>Sartre claims that human beings are "nothing but" what they make of themselves (<em>l'homme n'est rien d'autre que ce qu'il se fait</em>). What does this deny?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12a" id="q12a">
        <label for="q12a">That humans have emotions or bodily needs.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12b" id="q12b">
        <label for="q12b">That humans exist in a social world with other people.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12c" id="q12c">
        <label for="q12c">Any fixed human nature, divine purpose, biological destiny, or social role that determines who we fundamentally are.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12d" id="q12d">
        <label for="q12d">That humans can have consistent values over the course of a lifetime.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q13b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">13</span> <div><p>Sartre's concept of "facticity" refers to the given facts of one's situation — one's body, history, social position. How does facticity relate to freedom in his view?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13a" id="q13a">
        <label for="q13a">Facticity determines freedom: the more favorable your situation, the more free you are.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13b" id="q13b">
        <label for="q13b">Facticity is the raw material freedom acts upon — we cannot change the facts of our situation, but we are always free in how we respond to and interpret them.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13c" id="q13c">
        <label for="q13c">Facticity is an illusion — the self is entirely free from any constraints.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13d" id="q13d">
        <label for="q13d">Facticity replaces freedom: once one's situation is fully understood, the correct choice becomes apparent.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q14a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">14</span> <div><p>How does Sartre's existentialism differ from Hegel's view of freedom as developed in this course?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14a" id="q14a">
        <label for="q14a">For Hegel, freedom is realized collectively through history and institutions; for Sartre, freedom is always individual and cannot be delegated to any collective process.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14b" id="q14b">
        <label for="q14b">Hegel believes freedom is an illusion; Sartre believes it is real.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14c" id="q14c">
        <label for="q14c">Both Hegel and Sartre agree that individual freedom is the highest value, but differ on how to achieve it.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14d" id="q14d">
        <label for="q14d">Sartre agrees with Hegel's account of freedom but applies it to individual psychology rather than history.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q15b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">15</span> <div><p>Sartre argues that we cannot escape responsibility by appealing to passion — claiming we were "swept away" and couldn't help ourselves. Why not?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15a" id="q15a">
        <label for="q15a">Passions are not real — they are performances we put on to justify our choices after the fact.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15b" id="q15b">
        <label for="q15b">Because we are the ones who allow our passions to have authority over us; we choose to give them weight, and this choice is itself an exercise of freedom.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15c" id="q15c">
        <label for="q15c">Passions are morally irrelevant — only rational deliberation counts as genuine decision-making.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15d" id="q15d">
        <label for="q15d">Legal systems punish passion-driven acts as harshly as premeditated ones, and Sartre follows legal precedent.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <button class="quiz__submit" type="button">Check Answers</button>
  <div class="quiz__results"></div>
</div>
